CAT = "ccrnProf"; SEG = "PRO"
Q = [
# Advocacy and moral agency
("A nurse speaks up at rounds to make sure the team hears what a patient told her about his wishes. Which Synergy Model nurse competency is this?",
 "Advocacy and moral agency", ["Systems thinking in care", "Clinical inquiry", "Facilitation of learning"],
 "The Synergy Model defines advocacy and moral agency as working on another's behalf and representing the concerns of the patient, family and staff, including helping to resolve ethical and clinical concerns."),
("A nurse believes a patient's care plan is wrong but feels unable to act on it because of the hospital's constraints. What is the nurse experiencing?",
 "Moral distress", ["Compassion fatigue", "Role ambiguity", "Moral uncertainty"],
 "Moral distress is knowing the right thing to do while feeling constrained from doing it. Moral uncertainty is being unsure what is right, and compassion fatigue builds from long exposure to others' suffering."),
("The family of an alert, competent patient asks the team not to tell him about his new cancer diagnosis. What should the nurse do?",
 "Ask what he wants to know", ["Agree to keep it from him", "Tell him every detail at once", "Leave it all to the physician"],
 "A competent patient has the right to information about his own care, and some prefer to hear less or to have family present. Asking him how much he wants to know respects his autonomy and the family's concern."),
("A patient's daughter makes decisions by asking what her father would have chosen, based on what he said and valued. Which standard is she using?",
 "Substituted judgment", ["The best interest standard", "Medical paternalism", "Beneficence alone"],
 "Substituted judgment asks what the patient would decide if he could. The best interest standard is used when the patient's wishes are not known and asks what would most benefit him."),
("A patient with decision-making capacity refuses intubation after the risks are explained, and the team agrees he understands them. What should the nurse do?",
 "Respect the refusal", ["Ask the family to decide", "Intubate if SpO2 drops", "Seek a court order"],
 "A patient with capacity may refuse any treatment, even life-sustaining treatment. The refusal is documented, other comfort and support measures are offered, and the plan is revisited if the patient changes his mind."),
("After several meetings, a family and the team still disagree about continuing life support. What is the most appropriate next step?",
 "Request an ethics consultation", ["Transfer the patient elsewhere", "Withdraw support on schedule", "Limit the family's visits"],
 "An ethics consultation offers structured help to clarify values, options and who decides when conflict persists. It supports both the family and the team rather than imposing one side's view."),
# Caring practices
("A nurse keeps the room calm, anticipates the patient's discomfort and checks on him often. Which Synergy Model competency is this?",
 "Caring practices", ["Systems thinking", "Clinical judgment", "Response to diversity"],
 "Caring practices create a compassionate, supportive and therapeutic environment that promotes comfort and prevents unnecessary suffering, and they include vigilance, engagement and responsiveness."),
("A patient's wife asks to stay in the room during his resuscitation. What is the best response?",
 "Allow it with a support person", ["Ask her to wait outside the unit", "Allow it only once ROSC returns", "Allow it only if she stays silent"],
 "Family presence during resuscitation is supported when a staff member stays with the family to explain what is happening and to watch for distress. Many families find it helps them understand that everything was done."),
("A patient is dying on comfort care, and the monitor alarms keep sounding. What should the nurse do?",
 "Silence nonessential alarms", ["Keep full monitoring on", "Limit the family to 2 hours", "Continue the daily labs"],
 "At the end of life, care shifts to comfort: monitors and alarms that no longer change care are turned off or silenced, routine tests are stopped and the family is given time and privacy."),
("A frightened patient is about to have a bedside procedure. Which caring practice most reduces anxiety?",
 "Explain what he will feel", ["Begin without any warning", "Send the family home first", "Avoid long eye contact"],
 "Telling a patient what to expect, including sensations, and staying with him lowers anxiety and builds trust. Comfort measures such as positioning and pain control are part of the same plan."),
("A patient with chronic pain says his pain is 8 out of 10 but appears calm and is watching television. What should the nurse do?",
 "Accept his report of pain", ["Assume he is drug seeking", "Rate it lower in the chart", "Wait until he looks distressed"],
 "Self-report is the reference standard for pain in patients who can give it, and people with chronic pain often do not show distress. The nurse assesses, treats and reassesses."),
# Clinical inquiry
("A nurse questions a unit routine, searches the evidence and leads a change to practice. Which Synergy Model competency is this?",
 "Clinical inquiry", ["Caring practices", "Collaboration", "Advocacy and agency"],
 "Clinical inquiry is the ongoing process of questioning and evaluating practice and making informed changes through evidence-based practice, research and experience."),
("Which type of evidence is usually ranked highest for guiding a change in practice?",
 "Meta-analysis of RCTs", ["An expert panel opinion", "A small case series", "A single cohort study"],
 "A systematic review or meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials pools the strongest designs, so it sits at the top of most evidence hierarchies. Expert opinion and case series sit near the bottom."),
("In a PICO question, what does the C stand for?",
 "Comparison", ["Cost of care", "Consent", "Context"],
 "PICO is Population, Intervention, Comparison and Outcome. Framing a question this way makes the literature search focused and the answer usable."),
("A nurse finds that a unit's practice differs from current evidence. What is the best first step?",
 "Take the evidence to the unit council", ["Change her own practice quietly", "Tell new staff to ignore the policy", "Wait for an adverse event to occur"],
 "Practice change works best through the unit's shared governance or practice council, which reviews the evidence, updates the policy and plans education so everyone changes together."),
("Which group reviews a research study to protect its participants before it begins?",
 "Institutional review board", ["The ethics consult service", "The pharmacy committee", "The risk management office"],
 "An institutional review board checks that a study's risks are reasonable, that consent is informed and that participants are protected. An ethics consult service helps with individual patient care conflicts."),
("A unit wants to try a new turning schedule on a few patients, check the results and adjust before spreading it. Which method fits?",
 "A Plan-Do-Study-Act cycle", ["A root cause analysis", "A randomized controlled trial", "A failure mode analysis"],
 "PDSA cycles test small changes quickly and build on what is learned. A root cause analysis looks back at an event, and a failure mode analysis looks ahead for risks in a process."),
# Collaboration
("A nurse works with the respiratory therapist, pharmacist and family to set a realistic weaning goal. Which Synergy Model competency is this?",
 "Collaboration", ["Clinical inquiry", "Caring practices", "Systems thinking"],
 "Collaboration is working with others, including patients, families and other professionals, in a way that encourages each person's contribution toward optimal and realistic goals."),
("A nurse must call a physician about a patient whose condition is getting worse. Which tool structures the call?",
 "SBAR", ["SOAP", "PERRLA", "RACE"],
 "SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) gives a clear, brief structure for urgent communication. SOAP is a charting format, PERRLA a pupil check and RACE a fire response."),
("Which of these is one of the AACN standards for a healthy work environment?",
 "Skilled communication", ["Mandatory overtime", "Seniority-based pay", "Primary nursing models"],
 "AACN's six standards are skilled communication, true collaboration, effective decision making, appropriate staffing, meaningful recognition and authentic leadership."),
("A nurse is upset by a colleague's repeated comments at the nurses' station. What is the best first step?",
 "Talk with the colleague privately", ["Complain to other coworkers", "Avoid the colleague entirely", "Email the whole unit about it"],
 "Raising the concern directly and privately, using skilled communication, is the first step; it often resolves the problem and keeps the working relationship. Escalation follows if the behavior continues."),
("In team communication training, what does the phrase CUS stand for?",
 "Concerned, Uncomfortable, Safety", ["Calm, Understand, Support", "Check, Update, Sign, Send", "Communicate, Urgent, Stop"],
 "CUS is a set of escalating phrases: I am Concerned, I am Uncomfortable, this is a Safety issue. It gives any team member clear words to stop the line when a patient is at risk."),
("A physician gives a verbal order during a resuscitation. How does the nurse use closed-loop communication?",
 "Repeat the order back", ["Nod and give the drug", "Write it down later", "Ask another nurse to check"],
 "Closed-loop communication means the receiver repeats the order and the sender confirms it, then the receiver reports when it is done. It catches errors in drug, dose and route."),
("Which practice best involves the family in collaborative ICU care?",
 "Inviting them to daily rounds", ["Asking them to leave at rounds", "Sharing updates only at discharge", "Limiting them to set hours"],
 "Family participation in rounds keeps the family informed, lets them share what they know about the patient and builds trust in decisions. It also supports patient- and family-centered care."),
# Facilitation of learning
("A nurse uses plain language, pictures and practice sessions to teach a family about heart failure. Which Synergy Model competency is this?",
 "Facilitation of learning", ["Clinical inquiry", "Collaboration", "Advocacy and moral agency"],
 "Facilitation of learning is the ability to help patients, families, staff and the community learn, through both formal and informal teaching."),
("What is the best way to check that a family understands discharge instructions?",
 "Use teach-back", ["Ask if they have questions", "Give them a pamphlet", "Watch for nodding"],
 "In teach-back the family explains the instructions in their own words, which shows what they understood and lets the nurse correct gaps. A yes to 'Any questions?' does not show understanding."),
("A patient in severe pain needs teaching about his new drain. When should teaching happen?",
 "Once his pain is controlled", ["Right now, while he is alert", "Only on the day of discharge", "Only if he asks for it"],
 "Severe pain, anxiety and fatigue limit learning, so the pain is treated first and teaching is given in short sessions when the patient is ready."),
("Adult learners are most engaged when the content is:",
 "Relevant to current needs", ["Mostly abstract theory", "Delivered as a long lecture", "Unrelated to their tasks"],
 "Adults learn best when material solves a problem they face now and builds on their experience, so teaching is tied to what the patient or staff member needs to do next."),
("A new infusion pump is coming to the unit. Which method best confirms staff can use it safely?",
 "A return demonstration", ["A signed policy memo", "A short video alone", "A written quiz alone"],
 "Watching staff perform the task shows competence in a way that reading or a quiz cannot. Hands-on practice with a return demonstration is the usual standard for a new device."),
("At about what reading level should written patient education materials be written?",
 "Sixth to eighth grade", ["Twelfth grade or above", "College level", "Third grade or below"],
 "Many adults have limited health literacy, so plain language at about a sixth to eighth grade level, with short sentences and pictures, is advised. Teach-back confirms the reader understood."),
# Response to diversity
("A nurse adapts care to a family's religious practices and their own language. Which Synergy Model competency is this?",
 "Response to diversity", ["Systems thinking", "Clinical inquiry skills", "Caring practices"],
 "Response to diversity is the sensitivity to recognize, appreciate and include differences such as culture, spirituality, gender, ethnicity, lifestyle, socioeconomic status, age and values in care."),
("A patient speaks limited English. Who should interpret a conversation about consent for surgery?",
 "A qualified medical interpreter", ["The patient's teenage son", "A bilingual visitor nearby", "Gestures and picture boards"],
 "A trained interpreter, in person, by video or by phone, gives accurate and confidential interpretation. Family members, especially children, may leave things out or change meaning, and it puts them in a hard position."),
("An adult Jehovah's Witness with capacity refuses blood transfusion after a GI bleed. What should the nurse do?",
 "Respect it; discuss alternatives", ["Transfuse if he gets worse", "Ask the family to override", "Transfer him to another unit"],
 "A competent adult's refusal of blood is respected. The team discusses which blood alternatives and conservation measures he accepts, such as iron, erythropoietin and fewer blood draws, and documents his wishes."),
("A family wants to keep a religious amulet tied to the patient's wrist. What should the nurse do?",
 "Allow it if it is safe", ["Remove it for infection control", "Ask them to take it home", "Allow it only on one shift"],
 "Respecting cultural and spiritual practices supports the patient and family. The nurse works around the object, such as moving it for a procedure, unless it truly interferes with care."),
("A patient's family asks for a spiritual leader of their faith to visit at night. What is the best response?",
 "Help arrange the visit", ["Refuse after visiting hours", "Offer only the chaplain on duty", "Ask them to wait until morning"],
 "Meeting spiritual needs is part of holistic care. The nurse helps arrange the visit and gives privacy, and the chaplain service can help contact community faith leaders."),
# Systems thinking
("A nurse knows how to find resources across departments to get a patient home safely. Which Synergy Model competency is this?",
 "Systems thinking", ["Caring practices", "Clinical inquiry", "Response to diversity"],
 "Systems thinking is the body of knowledge and tools that lets the nurse manage the environmental and system resources that exist for the patient, family and staff, within or across health care systems."),
("When should discharge planning begin for a patient in the ICU?",
 "At admission", ["On the day of discharge", "After extubation", "When the family asks"],
 "Planning starts at admission so needs such as rehabilitation, equipment, teaching and follow-up are found early and the transition out of the ICU is safe."),
("A nurse catches a tenfold dosing error before it reaches the patient. What should the nurse do?",
 "Report it in the safety system", ["Fix it and tell no one", "Report it only if harm occurs", "Tell the prescriber only"],
 "Near misses are reported so the system can find and fix the weakness behind them. A just culture treats reporting as a way to improve safety, not as grounds for blame."),
("After a sentinel event, what does a root cause analysis focus on?",
 "Systems and processes", ["Whom to discipline", "The patient's own errors", "The cost of the event"],
 "A root cause analysis looks for the system and process failures that allowed the event, so changes can prevent it from happening again. It is not a search for someone to blame."),
("Nurses on a unit are ignoring frequent false monitor alarms. Which change best reduces alarm fatigue?",
 "Customize alarm limits", ["Turn the volumes down", "Keep default limits", "Turn off SpO2 alarms"],
 "Setting alarm limits and delays to the individual patient, changing electrodes daily and removing monitoring that is not needed cut false alarms so that real ones are heard. Silencing or ignoring alarms puts patients at risk."),
]
